codex
Implemented and verified a Track B generator based on Proposition 3’s native binary-matrix objects from the [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/1209.1842).

Shipping preset: 240 matrices, 32 independent roots, 80×80 root matrices.

- All G1–G9 gates pass.
- Structure-aware guessing: 0/200,000; exact probability \(2^{-32}\).
- Five attacks: 0/8 successes each.
- Reference algorithm: 205,008 bit/edge operations.
- Compact route: 271 operations.
- Oracle arms: bare 0/3, hinted 0/3, placebo 0/3.
- Answer: 242 serialized characters, 240 elements.

Deliverables:

- [gen_1209_1842.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1209.1842/gen_1209_1842.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1209.1842/selftest_report.json)
- [llm_loop_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1209.1842/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1209.1842/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1209.1842/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1209.1842/README.md)

The README explicitly notes that this covers the binary matrices used in the proof, not Cartesian-product graph instances themselves. Temporary hardening directories were moved to trash after their transcripts were preserved.
diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
